-   "change_vs_production": "Reference 4200-11,500/µL unchanged (Echevarne H0007). Optimal 4,500-6,000/µL → 4,200-6,000/µL: the floor moves to the laboratory's lower limit because production's 4,500 was a judgement with no source and no opened cohort places a threshold there (f12-E05, f12-E06, f12-E08; PQ-f12-02); the ceiling 6,000 is kept and now rests on the Women's Health Initiative (f12-E01, f12-E03) with f12-E04 and f12-E08. The below-optimal zone is no longer reachable and its message is listed for deletion. Critical limits 2,000 and 30,000/µL added from the draft critical-limits table (critical-limits.json entries[7]; the alternative 1,400/50,000 stays with the physician there). Action limit AL-14 added. Messages and note rewritten to the wording rules: production's out-of-range messages said 'prompt medical attention', its optimal and above-optimal messages spoke of cardiovascular and all-cause mortality, and none had the four elements or the safety-net sentence.",
-   "notes": "Class a, priority member of the leukocyte redundancy group and the only member scored (marker-classes.json draft): graded outcome associations in both sexes in cohorts of 160,117 to 686,475 adults (f12-E01, f12-E04, f12-E10, f12-E11, f12-E12; an earlier analysis of part of the first cohort, f12-E09; a small meta-analysis, f12-E15), a genetic study that supports a modest causal effect on coronary heart disease (f12-E13), and a modifiable cause, smoking (f12-E14). Band: within 10% of the minimum hazard ratio in the Women's Health Initiative (f12-E01), and the same in its reverse-causation table, which excludes comorbidity and starts follow-up at year 6 (minimum 0.95; 5,700-6,099 at 1.00; 6,100-6,499 at 1.05, just above the 10% limit of 1.045; f12-V01); floor at the laboratory's lower limit (f12-E05, f12-E06). Sex: the split is Echevarne's (4,200 men, 3,800 women); the outcome band has the same ceiling in both sexes (no interaction with sex in f12-E04); published sex differences are not consistent with the laboratory's lower floor for women (f12-E19; PQ-f12-10). Age: no split; the direction is the same at 30-45 and at 60 and over (f12-E07), in men of 64 to 84 (f12-E17) and at 85 (f12-E16), and healthy adults of 60 and over needed no separate reference interval (f12-E18); no lowest-risk level specific to 85 and over was opened. Pregnancy and lactation (does not apply to this row; recorded as in the women's row): app rule (state-rules.md 2.1: laboratory interval only, no category, fixed note); the count rises in pregnancy to an upper reference limit of about 15,000/µL (f12-E56, f12-E57) and returns to pre-pregnancy levels by day 21 after delivery, so no state row is written (PQ-f12-08); the critical limits stay on. Menopause, hormone therapy and oral contraception: no flag; state-rules.md section 4 names no effect on the white cell count and no study was found (f12-E55 for the differential). Population check: in the programme's NHANES healthy adults (input $.markers.white_blood_cell_count.nhanes) the shares inside 4,500-6,000 and below it give, for this row's band, 28.6% to 29.5% optimal at ages 20-79 in 2015-2018 and 28.0% to 29.1% in 1999-2006; at 70-79 the non-optimal share is 59.6% (women) and 70.0% (men) in 2015-2018 and 72.3% and 72.4% in 1999-2006, above the 60% that check N1 sends to the physician (PQ-f12-01). The reference interval leaves 4.4% to 7.5% of those adults outside in 2015-2018 and 3.2% to 6.8% in 1999-2006, inside the 2% to 15% rule."
