    "messages": {
     "en": {
      "critical_low": "Your white blood cell count is far below the reference interval, under 2,000/µL. With so few white cells the body is much less able to fight infections, and a fever can be the only warning sign. Some medicines, infections and disorders of the bone marrow can cause it. This value is beyond the critical limit set by EVA's responsible physician. Contact a doctor today; if you cannot, go to an emergency department. This result is not a diagnosis.",
      "below_range": "Your white blood cell count is below the laboratory's reference interval, which starts at 3,800/µL for women. White cells defend the body against infections; a count a little under the interval is common and often has no consequence. It often falls for a while after a viral infection, some medicines recorded in your context can lower it, and in many people of African or Middle Eastern descent a lower count is a harmless inherited variant (Duffy-null phenotype). Repeat the test in 2 to 4 weeks to confirm it, and look at your neutrophil and lymphocyte counts, which have their own messages. If it is still low, book an appointment with your doctor in the next few weeks, sooner if you have fever or repeated infections. This result is not a diagnosis. Discuss it with your doctor, who knows your history. If you feel unwell, do not wait for your next appointment.",
-     "optimal": "Your white blood cell count is in EVA's optimal band (3,800 to 6,000/µL for women). In large population studies, adults whose count was in this lower part of the reference interval had the best long-term health.",
