-   "change_vs_production": "The marker is not in the production snapshot of 25 September; its two rows were inserted that day by migration 20260925012000_nlr_row (18-59: reference 0.8-3.5; 60+: 0.8-3.9; optimal 0.8-2.0 in both). Against those rows: (1) age bands 18-59 and 60+ become 18-64, 65-74, 75-84 and 85+, the bands of the source of the limits (f12-E39 to f12-E42); (2) upper reference limit 3.5 → 3.53, copied exactly (f12-E38, f12-E39); (3) lower reference limit 0.8 and optimal floor 0.8 removed: a low ratio carried no excess in the general population (f12-E46) and the limit fell on healthy people with a benign low neutrophil count (f12-E53); the below-range message is no longer reachable and is listed for deletion; (4) optimal ceiling 2.0 kept, proposed at grade C for the grade C decision table (PQ-f12-05). Messages and note rewritten to the wording rules: the note and three messages spoke of mortality or survival (known issue C-27).",
-   "notes": "Class a with a grade C band, not scored (PQ-f12-05). Optimal ceiling 2.0: the edge of the lowest-risk category in 835,430 Danish adults followed for a median of 11.2 years (f12-E45, f12-E47), with an older Dutch cohort placing it lower: crude survival differed from the third quintile, 1.60-1.91 (f12-E49), and the adjusted all-cause hazard ratios by quartile were 1.05, 1.13 and 1.59 against the lowest (f12-V02), so the within-10% edge there is the cohort median, which lies between 1.60 and 1.91. Proposed grade C: the category was fixed in advance, the association fades with time since the sample (f12-E50, f12-E51) and a genetic study finds no causal effect on coronary disease (f12-E52); under PLAN section 5 a grade C band narrower than the interval needs a grade C decision-table entry, which the decision register already foresees (DR-2026-09-25-16), hence grade_c_table_entry. If the physician declines the entry, the row shows in range or above range only (class e). Reference: population-based upper limit for this age band, f12-E38, f12-E39; no lower limit. Age: this row covers 18 to 64; under 45 the only opened source is f12-E38 (21 to 66 years). The optimal ceiling is the same at every age because no opened outcome study gives age-specific categories; the mean ratio is 2.02 at 75-84 and 2.13 at 85 and over (f12-E41, f12-E42), so at those ages about half or more of people read above optimal; the above-optimal message says that the finding is common and that the ratio rises with age. Sex: no split; the ratio is higher in men overall (mean 1.88 against 1.68 in f12-E39's source) and the sex difference reverses around age 50 (f12-E55), with no sex-specific outcome band. Pregnancy and lactation: app rule (state-rules.md 2.1); the ratio averages 2.6, 4.0 and 3.5 in the three trimesters (f12-E54), so the non-pregnant limits do not hold in pregnancy and no state row is written (PQ-f12-08); the messages of this age band name pregnancy as a cause of a higher ratio. Menopause, hormone therapy and oral contraception: no flag (f12-E55). Coherence: a ratio can lie above its range while both counts lie inside Echevarne's wide intervals, which check K1 counts as an opposite pair (PQ-f12-07). NHANES: the programme's file has no NLR, so check N1 is not run; US adults average 2.15 (f12-E53)."
+   "change_vs_production": "The marker is not in the production snapshot of 25 September; its two rows were inserted that day by migration 20260925012000_nlr_row (18-59: reference 0.8-3.5; 60+: 0.8-3.9; optimal 0.8-2.0 in both). Against those rows: (1) age bands 18-59 and 60+ become 18-64, 65-74, 75-84 and 85+, the bands of the source of the limits (f12-E39 to f12-E42); (2) upper reference limit 3.5 → 3.53, copied exactly (f12-E38, f12-E39); (3) lower reference limit 0.8 removed: a low ratio carried no excess in the general population (f12-E46; no nonlinear association and no excess in the lowest quartile in NHANES, f12-F04, f12-F05) and the limit fell on healthy people with a benign low neutrophil count (f12-E53); the below-range message is no longer reachable and is listed for deletion; optimal floor 0.8 → 0.37 (round 2): the lowest ratio two counts inside Echevarne's intervals can give, so that a ratio only an out-of-range count can produce no longer reads optimal (confirmed ledger issue K1; f12-F01, f12-F02); (4) optimal ceiling 2.0 kept, proposed at grade C for the grade C decision table (PQ-f12-05). Messages and note rewritten to the wording rules: the note and three messages spoke of mortality or survival (known issue C-27). Round 2 (fixer, 2026-11-10): the optimal and above-optimal messages and the note no longer restate mortality findings ('best long-term health', 'somewhat poorer long-term health'; confirmed ledger issue M3), and a below-optimal message is added for the new floor.",
+   "notes": "Class a with a grade C band, not scored (PQ-f12-05). Optimal ceiling 2.0: the edge of the lowest-risk category in 835,430 Danish adults followed for a median of 11.2 years (f12-E45, f12-E47), with an older Dutch cohort placing it lower: crude survival differed from the third quintile, 1.60-1.91 (f12-E49), and the adjusted all-cause hazard ratios by quartile were 1.05, 1.13 and 1.59 against the lowest (f12-V02), so the within-10% edge there is the cohort median, which lies between 1.60 and 1.91. Proposed grade C: the category was fixed in advance, the association fades with time since the sample (f12-E50, f12-E51) and a genetic study finds no causal effect on coronary disease (f12-E52); under PLAN section 5 a grade C band narrower than the interval needs a grade C decision-table entry, which the decision register already foresees (DR-2026-09-25-16), hence grade_c_table_entry. If the physician declines the entry, the row shows in range or above range only (class e). Reference: population-based upper limit for this age band, f12-E38, f12-E39; no lower limit. Age: this row covers 18 to 64; under 45 the only opened source is f12-E38 (21 to 66 years). The optimal ceiling is the same at every age because no opened outcome study gives age-specific categories; the mean ratio is 2.02 at 75-84 and 2.13 at 85 and over (f12-E41, f12-E42), so at those ages about half or more of people read above optimal; the above-optimal message says that the finding is common and that the ratio rises with age. Sex: no split; the ratio is higher in men overall (mean 1.88 against 1.68 in f12-E39's source) and the sex difference reverses around age 50 (f12-E55), with no sex-specific outcome band. Pregnancy and lactation: app rule (state-rules.md 2.1); the ratio averages 2.6, 4.0 and 3.5 in the three trimesters (f12-E54), so the non-pregnant limits do not hold in pregnancy and no state row is written (PQ-f12-08); the messages of this age band name pregnancy as a cause of a higher ratio. Menopause, hormone therapy and oral contraception: no flag (f12-E55). Coherence: a ratio can lie above its range while both counts lie inside Echevarne's wide intervals, which check K1 counts as an opposite pair (PQ-f12-07). NHANES: the programme's file has no NLR, so check N1 is not run; US adults average 2.15 (f12-E53). Round 2 (fixer, 2026-11-10): optimal floor 0.37 (F12-DQ-1): with it, neutrophils 375/µL and lymphocytes 5,330/µL (ratio 0.07) read below optimal instead of optimal; the remaining K1 pattern on this row, a ratio above the age limit with both counts inside their wide intervals, cannot be removed by a band the evidence supports (F12-DQ-2, PQ-f12-07)."
